    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential tremor (G25.0)</content>. An isolated, bilateral action tremor of the upper limbs, present for at least three years by history and worse over six months, without head, voice, or leg tremor or any other neurological sign: essential tremor by the MDS 1608 criteria. TSH 1.6 mIU/L, glucose 92 mg/dL, calcium 9.4 mg/dL, sodium 139 mmol/L, and creatinine 0.96 mg/dL exclude thyroid and metabolic causes, and he takes no tremor-inducing medicine. Alcohol rebound and caffeine add an enhanced physiological component, and the brief improvement after a drink is typical of essential tremor. There are no signs of Parkinson disease. Dr. Osei-Carrow agrees. TETRAS performance 22 and activities of daily living 18; writing item 3. [S]<list><item>Propranolol extended-release 60 mg each morning, starting today; increase by response at four weeks.</item><item>Occupational therapy for writing.</item><item>Do not use alcohol to treat the tremor; keep to the agreed limits.</item><item>Handwriting sample and spiral at each visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tremor of the writing hand (R25.1)</content>. Classified as essential tremor; superseded by that diagnosis. [S]<list><item>Managed under essential tremor.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hazardous drinking (Z72.89)</content>. About 14 drinks a week and no more than four on any day after the brief intervention, from about 24. No features of an alcohol use disorder or withdrawal. GGT 84 U/L and MCV 99 fL at intake fit regular heavy drinking; ALT 41 and AST 44 U/L are at the upper end of normal. [S]<list><item>Goal: no more than 14 drinks a week and no more than four on any day, with alcohol-free days; he aims for about 10.</item><item>Recheck GGT and liver tests in eight to ten weeks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Tremor rating: TETRAS performance and activities of daily living subscales</td><td>At each visit while treatment is adjusted</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Performance 22 of 64 and activities of daily living 18 of 48 (rated by neurology today); writing item 3</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Archimedes spiral and handwriting sample (the same sentence each time, dated and kept in the chart)</td><td>At each visit while treatment is adjusted</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Right spiral wide and jagged with a clear tremor axis; left mildly irregular; handwriting large, jagged, and drifting upward</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Examination for signs beyond tremor (rest tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, dystonia, ataxia, head or voice tremor)</td><td>At diagnosis and when the tremor changes</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None found; isolated action tremor of both upper limbs</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH and metabolic tests to exclude other causes</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Results from the first visit reviewed today: TSH, glucose, calcium, sodium, and creatinine normal</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Drugs and substances that worsen tremor (beta-agonists, valproate, lithium, antidepressants, thyroid hormone, decongestants, caffeine, alcohol rebound)</td><td>At diagnosis and at each medication change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No medicines; caffeine cut to three cups; alcohol reduced</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Baseline before a beta-blocker: resting pulse, seated and standing blood pressure, asthma, heart block, diabetes</td><td>Before starting</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pulse 78; blood pressure 130/82 mmHg with no postural drop; no asthma, heart block, or diabetes</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Drinking review after the brief intervention (drinks a week, heaviest day, use of alcohol to steady the hand)</td><td>At each visit after a brief intervention</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About 14 drinks a week, no day above four, from about 24; no withdrawal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>GGT and liver tests after reducing alcohol</td><td>Eight to twelve weeks after the brief intervention</td><td>Due</td><td>GGT 84 U/L at intake; recheck in eight to ten weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Movement disorders neurology consultation</td><td>At diagnosis if the tremor type is uncertain or treatment is planned</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; essential tremor confirmed; propranolol advised</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Occupational therapy for writing</td><td>When tremor limits writing or daily tasks</td><td>Referred</td><td>Referred today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Primidone (sedation, unsteadiness, nausea)</td><td>Only if used</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not started; propranolol is effective and tolerated</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Brain MRI or dopamine transporter imaging</td><td>Only for atypical features (rest tremor, parkinsonism, asymmetry with other signs, sudden onset, ataxia)</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No atypical features; the diagnosis is clinical</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Deep brain stimulation or focused ultrasound thalamotomy</td><td>Only for disabling tremor despite adequate trials of two first-line drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Tremor responding to the first drug</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Classify the tremor and exclude treatable causes by the second visit. Outcome by 1616-01-26: Essential tremor diagnosed; TSH, glucose, calcium, and kidney tests normal; no parkinsonism [S]</item><item>Legible handwriting for a full page and a steady signature. In progress; target 1616-04-20 [S]</item><item>No more than 14 standard drinks a week and no more than 4 on any day. In progress; target 1616-04-20 [S]</item><item>Resting pulse above 55 without dizziness, wheeze, or fatigue on propranolol. In progress; target 1616-04-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline update: treatment of essential tremor: report of the Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology (2011; reaffirmed 2025) (2011); Consensus Statement on the classification of tremors, from the task force on tremor of the International Parkinson and Movement Disorder Society (2018); MDS evidence-based review of treatments for essential tremor (2019); Unhealthy Alcohol Use in Adolescents and Adults: Screening and Behavioral Counseling Interventions (2018); Helping Patients Who Drink Too Much: A Clinician&apos;s Guide (2005); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database (no essential tremor monograph; the Parkinson&apos;s disease monograph informs the exclusion of parkinsonism) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Four-week review of propranolol with a tremor rating, pulse, and blood pressure; preventive visit in three weeks as booked. Next scheduled visit 1616-02-16: preventive health visit at 51. [S]</paragraph></text>

